Organization = {Global Network Environm Sci \& Technol; Univ Aegean, Dept Environm
   Studies},
Abstract = {The European Water Framework Directive (2000/60/E.U.) is the cornerstone
   of environmental legislation in the E.U. and to some extent an exemplar
   of advanced environmental thinking. It establishes a new legislative
   framework for the sustainable management of all waters, groundwater and
   surface waters and the protection of their relevant ecosystems. It does
   not simply assign standards of water protection, but also outlines the
   organisational and procedural aspects of water management. Furthermore,
   it accomplishes to harmonise and update the existing water legislation
   and establishes a coherent framework for the sustainable management of
   water resources. However, the implementation of the Water Framework
   Directive raises several technical, administrative and scientific issues
   in particular due to the extremely demanding timetable of the nine
   preparatory years and the complexity of its text, especially of the
   technical Annexes. Hence, a challenge exists in describing the basic
   requirements of the Directive and in analysing and explaining its main
   issues so as to clarify its principles and assist the successful
   transition from the general definitions to practical guidance and
   effective implementation. Moreover, the imminent application of the
   Directive is considered to be particularly intricate in a number of
   Member States where the structure of the environmental sector was
   significantly different than the one proposed by the Directive. Such is
   the case of Greece, where the natural water environment and
   hydro-morphology as well as the current institutional and organisational
   framework cause substantial difficulties in the procedure of
   implementation. The paper focuses on the geographical allocation of
   water catchments and their present condition as well as the
   administrative and legislative framework of the country. Through a
   critical assessment of the current situation in Greece, the key issues
   that are expected to emerge during the endeavour of the Directive's
   implementation are identified. Major challenges in all sectors,
   political, social and economical, have to be faced in order to achieve
   an effective implementation of the Directive in Greece. It is
   recommended that urgent measures and programmes be enforced in order to
   cover the deficiencies of the technical infrastructure and the adoption
   of a more centralised administrative model, similar to the UK
   Environment Agency, is proposed that may assist in overcoming current
   administrative problems. Finally, weaknesses of the legislative
   framework and social perceptions that need to change in order to
   accomplish a sufficient adoption of the Directive's principles and
   requirements are identified and discussed.},
