Pages = {37-44},
Note = {Asian Waterqual 95: The 5th IAWQ Asian Regional Conference on Water
   Quality and Pollution Control, MANILA, PHILIPPINES, FEB 07-09, 1995},
Organization = {Int Assoc Water Qual (IAWQ); Philippine Soc Sanitary Engn; IAWQ
   Philippine Natl Comm},
Abstract = {Conventionally organic carbon is assumed to be the limiting nutrient of
   regrowth. Inorganic phosphorus in comparison to carbon should be a good
   candidate for regrowth control because it can be measured and
   controlled. It may be able to limit regrowth in drinking water
   distribution systems. In order to clarify whether inorganic phosphorus
   can really control or limit regrowth, three kinds of bioassays were
   performed: 1. for different combinations of acetate, phosphorus, and
   other inorganic nutrients added to different test tubes containing pure
   water, 2. for different tap water dilutions with pure water, and 3. for
   different phosphorus addition to test tubes containing all nutrients but
   phosphorus. The inoculum used was taken from a drinking water
   distribution system carrying chlorinated groundwater. Results indicated
   the following:1. tap water bacteria cannot grow on simple organic carbon
   source (acetate) alone, 2. not organic carbon but inorganic nutrients
   limited the growth in tap water, and 3. tap water bacterial growth was
   suppressed when phosphorus was not present and it was not affected by
   over-presence of phosphorus when carbon or other nutrients became
   limiting. This gave a clue that phosphorus might play a major role in
   controlling regrowth in drinking water distribution system. (C) 1997
   IAWQ. Published by Elsevier Science Ltd.},
