Note = {IWA Asia-Pacific Regional Conference, FUKUOKA, JAPAN, SEP 12-15, 2001},
Organization = {IWA},
Abstract = {Recently, water pollution with high concentrations of organic matter has
   occurred frequently in Hakata Bay. It is said that a high level of
   primary production provides much. organic matter and affects water
   quality of the bay, and it is proved by the appearance of phytoplankton
   throughout the year. In this study, we simulated variations of
   phytoplankton population with a box-type model using monthly data in a
   long-term measurement and we analyzed the final growth rate changes of
   phytoplanktons that explain the conditions for its appearance.
   Consequently, we found that the final growth rate varies with pattern of
   half-year periodicity and water temperature and/or ambient nutrient
   controls the final growth rate to be low every January and July.},
