   China); Minist Environm Protect China, Natl Ctr Solid Waste Management;
   Chinese Soc Environm Sci, Solid Waste Branch},
Abstract = {Due to rapid economic growth, faster upgrade of electrical and
   electronic equipment causes the ever-increasing quantity of waste
   electrical and electronic products (WEEE). At present, China is not only
   a large consumption nation of electrical products, but also a largest
   importer of e-waste. Through reviewing the present status of e-waste
   industry and the legal system in China, this article analyzing several
   problems and barriers in China's e-waste recycling industry: (a) illegal
   e-waste import from developed countries; (b) the booming development of
   China's informal recycling sectors; (c) informal disposal leading to
   serious environmental problems and human health impacts; (d) formal
   companies facing with the problems of severe lack of e-waste; (e) the
   morbidity of China's regulatory and legal system. What's more, compared
   with several developed countries in e-waste management, this paper also
   recommends that (a) building up the regulatory system of e-waste
   management in both central and local government; (b) encouraging the
   development of formal disposal of WEEE; (c) forcing enterprises to focus
   on Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR). (C) 2012 Selection and/or
   peer-review under responsibility of Basel Convention Coordinating Centre
   for Asia and the Pacific and National Center of Solid Waste Management,
   Ministry of Environmental Protection of China.},
