@inproceedings{ WOS:A1996BH13D00007,
Author = {Kawamoto, K and Sakuma, S},
Book-Group-Author = {INT SOLID WASTE ASSOC},
Title = {A new technological trend for the disposal of kitchen waste in Japan},
Booktitle = {7TH ISWA INTERNATIONAL CONGRESS, PROCEEDINGS I},
Year = {1996},
Pages = {I60-I67},
Note = {7th ISWA International Congress and Exhibition, YOKOHAMA, JAPAN, OCT
   27-NOV 01, 1996},
Organization = {Int Solid Waste Assoc},
Abstract = {A new technological trend in Japan in recent years to reduce domestic
   refuse, especially kitchen waste, was introduced and discussed. The
   major aspect of the trend is the development of various machines for
   reducing or decomposing kitchen waste. The distinctive feature of the
   machines is that they are powered by electricity.
   Methods used in the machines can be classified into several types: 1)
   Combustion type using electric heating or microwave irradiation; 2)
   Drying type using hot-air generated by an electric heater; 3)
   Centrifugal force dewatering type; 4) Disintegration type combined with
   the collection of solids in suspended liquid; 5) Cold storage type, and
   6) Biological decomposition type. This biological type can be further
   classified into two types; one is the essential aerobic decomposition
   mode with mechanical mixing and the other is composting mode. The extent
   of reduction in both the volume and weight of waste differs in each
   mode. Types Nos.2 and 6 have entered the market in greater abundance
   than any of the other types. Furthermore, new waste treatment systems
   for use in housing area are emerging. These are the vertical transport
   system using electromotive machines, and the combination system of
   disposer and wastewater treatment facility in housing areas.
   Public awareness regarding domestic refuse and the new machines was
   investigated by a questionnaire. The results suggested that perceptions
   of kitchen waste differed from one age to another. Among the machines,
   both the drying type and accelerated aerobic biodegradation type were
   relatively well evaluated. The acknowledgment of the machines was less
   than 40\%. Some problems of waste disposal in the home were also
   demonstrated.
   A further survey of local governments was performed in order to grasp
   the fever of recognition and support for the machines. The results
   showed that support for the new machines still remain slim as a whole
   and that local governments supported kitchen waste disposers financially
   in many cases. It was noticed that how the governments which contribute
   to each purchaser with relatively a large amounts of financial support
   would affect the scene of waste disposal.
   The new type machines and systems are expected to contribute to the
   reduction of domestic refuse. We investigated the effects of
   wide-spreading of the machines. A trial estimation of economic effect of
   the machines in a series of MSW treatment system was performed by
   calculating cost in three stages, that were collection and transport of
   MSW, intermediate treatment and final treatment. The result showed that
   the reduction of certain amount of money produced by wide-spreading of
   the machines was predicted and that the cost of collection and transport
   process exceedingly contributed to the cost reduction.},
