   03, 1996},
Organization = {Psychon Soc; Natl Sci Fdn; USN, Off Naval Res},
Abstract = {M. Coltheart and K. Rastle (1991) reported that the sits of the
   regularity effect on word-naming latency decreases across position of
   irregularity, implicating a serial process in reading aloud. In response
   to criticism by D. C. Plant I. L. McClelland, hi, S. Seidenberg, and K.
   Patterson (1996), these results were replicated with monosyllabic words
   that bad been controled for consistency. In a second experiment,
   participants named nonword- or regular-word targets mired with either
   first-position irregular fillers or third-position irregular tillers.
   Target naming was slowed when first-position irregular fillers were
   present, compared with target naming when third-position irregular
   tillers K ere present. These data suggest that participants can slow use
   of the nonlexical route if faced with very costly exception words.
   Simulations using the dual-route cascaded model (M. Coltheart, B.
   Curtis, P.Atkins, \& M. Haller, 1993) are presented.},
