Note = {1st International Conference on Sustainable Urbanization (ICSU), Hong
   Kong, PEOPLES R CHINA, DEC 15-17, 2010},
Organization = {Hong Kong Polytechn Univ, Fac Construct \& Land Use; Inst Urban
   Environm; Road King Infrastructure Ltd; Sun Hung Kai Properties; Chun Wo
   Dev Holdings Ltd; K C Wong Educ Fdn; Environm \& Conservat Fund; Gammon;
   Paul Y; Architecture Design \& Res Grp Ltd (AD+RG); China State
   Construct (CSCEC); Sino Grp; Vantage Int (Holdings) Ltd; ASCE Hong Kong
   Sect; ASHRAE Hong Kong Chapter; CIBSE; Construct Ind Council (CIC);
   Chartered Inst Bldg (CIOB); Council Sustainable Dev; Emerald; Hong Kong
   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc},
Abstract = {This paper aims to develop an understanding of the major risks
   associated with green retail building development. The research
   methodologies comprised literature review, questionnaire survey and case
   study. Twelve major risks faced by `design and construct' (D\&C)
   contractors in designing and delivering Green Star rated retail building
   projects were identified and assessed. These risks include: limit of two
   submission attempts for Green Star certification set by the Green
   Building Council of Australia (GBCA); fail to assemble an adequate
   design and construction team; subcontractors and consultants lack
   experience with Green Star rating system; client project scope or
   objective changes affect achievement of Green Star Certified Rating;
   lack of coordination between project participants; contractors' project
   team lack experience in the Green Star rating system; clients' project
   criteria are not consistent with Green Star certification and
   performance goals; Green Star requirements are not included in project
   planning; contract award process is inadequate for green building
   requirements; contract conditions bind contractor to determined Green
   Star design rating; inadequate or insufficient site investigation; and
   the rating tool is misinterpreted or unclear in its intent. Strategies
   to manage these risks are presented in a companion paper Risk Management
   Strategies in Green Retail Building Development.},
