Note = {1st International Conference on Sustainable Urbanization (ICSU), Hong
   Kong Polytechn Univ, Fac Construct \& Environm, Hong Kong, PEOPLES R
   CHINA, DEC 15-17, 2010},
Organization = {Hong Kong Polytechn Univ, Fac Construct \& Land Use; Inst Urban
   Environm; Road King Infrastructure Ltd; Sun Hung Kai Properties; Chun Wo
   Dev Holdings Ltd; K C Wong Educ Fdn; Environm \& Conservat Fund; Gammon;
   Paul Y; Architecture Design \& Res Grp Ltd (AD+RG); China State
   Construct (CSCEC); Sino Grp; Vantage Int (Holdings) Ltd; ASCE Hong Kong
   Sect; ASHRAE Hong Kong Chapter; CIBSE; Construct Ind Council (CIC);
   Chartered Inst Bldg (CIOB); Council Sustainable Dev; Emerald; Hong Kong
   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc;
   Hong Kong Inst Util Specialists},
Abstract = {Disposal of electronic waste has become a major environmental concern in
   recent times. This waste is being generated at an alarming rate due to
   rapid advances in technology and an ever growing demand for new
   features. The most common type of electronic waste is Compact Discs
   (CD), which are widely used for data storage. CD recycling is therefore
   crucial to minimize the amount of waste requiring disposal. In this
   research, using waste CDs as recycled materials in concrete is a new
   concept. Waste CDs are simply cut into regular pieces and then mixed
   with concrete in the form of shreds. The purpose of this research was to
   investigate the fracture behaviour of concrete containing waste CD
   shreds using the three-point bending notched beam test, according to
   RILEM recommendations. The main parameters studied include CD shreds'
   content and size. The study indicates that the fracture energy (G(f))
   and modified characteristic length (l(ch,mod)) were found to increase
   significantly with increasing proportion and size of CD shreds due to
   anchoring and bridging effects. In other words, concrete with higher
   amounts and larger sizes of CD shreds exhibit higher cracking resistance
   and the brittleness decreases accordingly.},
Publisher = {HONG KONG POLYTECHNIC UNIV, FAC CONSTRUCTION \& ENVIRONMENT},
