Pages = {20-31},
Month = {JUL},
Note = {11th International Medical Geography Symposium, Ft Worth, TX, JUL, 2005},
Abstract = {Much attention is focused on obesity by both the media and by public
   health. As a health risk, obesity is recognised as a contributing factor
   to numerous health problems. Recent evidence points to a growth in
   levels of obesity in many countries and particular attention is usually
   given to rising levels of obesity among younger people. England is no
   exception to these generalisations with recent studies revealing a clear
   geography to what has been termed an `obesity epidernic.' This paper
   examines the complexities inherent in the geography of adult obesity in
   England. Existing knowledge about the subnational geography of obesity
   is examined and assessed. Multilevel synthetic estimation is then used
   to construct an age-sex-ethnicity disaggregated geography of obesity.
   These differing geographies are compared and contrasted with preexisting
   findings and explored at multiple scales. A complex picture of the
   geography of obesity in England is revealed. (C) 2007 Elsevier Ltd. All
   rights reserved.},
