   EDINBURGH, SCOTLAND, SEP, 2001},
Organization = {European Soc Cognitive Psych},
Abstract = {K. Rastle and M. Coltheart (1999) demonstrated that both nonwords and
   low-frequency regular words are named more slowly when mixed with
   first-phoneme irregular word fillers (e.g., CHEF) than when mixed with
   third-phoneme irregular word fillers (e.g., GLOW). Those authors
   suggested that their effects were due to a strategic de-emphasis of the
   nonlexical route when first-phoneme irregular fillers were used. An
   alternative explanation is that these results simply reflect a more lax
   position of a time criterion (S. J. Lupker, P. Brown, \& L. Colombo,
   1997) in the first-phoneme irregular filler condition. We contrasted
   these 2 accounts in 4 experiments. In all experiments, target naming
   latencies were longer when the fillers were harder to name, regardless
   of whether the fillers were nonwords or exception words. These results
   strongly favor a time-criterion account of K. Rastle and M. Coltheart's
   effects.},
