Note = {International Symposium on Sustainable-Development-of-City-Group,
   Changsha, PEOPLES R CHINA, NOV 16, 2009},
Organization = {Sustainable Dev City Grp; Cent S Univ, Hunan Inst Syst Engn \&
   Management},
Abstract = {Sanya is the most important tourist destination in Hainan Province,
   China. Tourism in the area has grown rapidly in recent years and is
   expected to further increase in the future. Every year millions of
   people flock to this tropical coastal city, with the number expected to
   reach 16 million people per year by 2020. Calculations in this study
   reveals that the daily per capita water demand of Sanya tourists is
   995.8 liters, 4.3 times as much as that of local residents (227 liters).
   Further calculations suggests tourism industry has become one of the
   major freshwater consumers by consuming 20.1\% of the city's total water
   supply. The over-use of tourists has put additional pressure on the
   freshwater resources. In addition, the city's tourism on-season just
   coincides with the time when natural water resource availability is at
   its lowest. Storing water (rainfall) in dams is the solutions to
   satisfying the ever-increasing thirst for water by ever-increasing
   numbers of tourists. As a consequence, tourism is making a major
   contribution to the unbalance of freshwater supply and demand. The
   results could be that the tourism becomes unsustainable. To establish a
   water-saving tourism industry is the key to restore and maintain the
   balance. Based on an overview of the current impact of tourism on
   freshwater resources and the underlying causes in Sanya, a precautionary
   water-management for reducing water consumption regarding the tourism
   industry and governments are given.},
