@inproceedings{ WOS:000169198100005,
Author = {Berry, A},
Editor = {Rotherham, ID},
Title = {Conservation, culture and conflict: managing degraded mires},
Booktitle = {PEATLAND ECOLOGY AND ARCHAEOLOGY: MANAGEMENT OF A CULTURAL LANDSCAPE},
Series = {LANDSCAPE ARCHAEOLOGY AND ECOLOGY},
Year = {1997},
Volume = {4},
Pages = {52-54},
Note = {Conference of the Landscape-Conservation-Forum, SHEFFIELD, ENGLAND, 1997},
Organization = {Sheffield Hallam Univ; Landscape Conservat Forum},
Abstract = {Spectacular finds of prehistoric trackways and bog bodies preserved
   within the peat, together with palaeo-environmental evidence in the form
   of pollen and plant remains providing important proxy-climate data and
   enabling the reconstruction of past environments both upon and
   surrounding our lowland raised mires have, perhaps understandably,
   provided the focus for our perceptions of the historic and cultural
   dimensions of peatlands.
   In striving for conservation of our mires, and the cultural resource
   contained therein, it is all too easy to overlook the historic
   landscapes arising from the processes of peat extraction itself. Yet
   intensive exploitation of peat as a resource is of widespread occurrence
   from the early medieval period, can be clearly identified from the Roman
   period in some areas (e.g. the Fens) and, it is postulated, may date to
   the prehistoric in others (e.g. Somerset Levels). The mosaic of peat
   cuttings may themselves subsequently be deemed as landscapes of high
   scenic and nature conservation value (e.g. The Broads).
   An alliance between archaeologists and other environmentalists seems
   natural. The aspirations of the nature conservation lobby and the
   techniques deployed in their achievement must, after all, do much to
   ensure preservation of the cultural resource within the peat. However,
   what of the impacts upon the wider cultural and historic landscapes of
   our mires? Do the statutory remits of the key agencies (English Nature,
   English Heritage, etc.) enable effective recognition and management of
   such issues? From an historic landscape perspective, this presentation
   considers the wider dimensions of the cultural resource of lowland
   raised mires, and addresses the pros and cons of the techniques of
   management and rehabilitation. It identifies areas of mutual
   compatibility and also of potential conflict between nature conservation
   and archaeological interests.
   Indeed there are some serious problems and potential ironies in the way
   that recent conservation campaigns have influenced the peat extraction
   industry. Protection of some sites, and the threat of protection for
   others, combined with pressure in the retail market from peat
   alternatives, has led to a drive to more rapid and intensive extraction
   from actively worked sites. Apparent determination by the industry to
   drive down retail prices and thus undercut the trade of competitors in
   the non-peat sector, has further exacerbated this effect. This has
   unfortunately resulted in dramatically intensive exploitation by
   open-cast peat mining, almost totally destroying large areas of raised
   mire, particularly around areas such as the Solway Firth on the Scottish
   borders. Accidentally, the conservation campaign may have actually
   speeded the demise of these sites.
   This is a view based on studies and experience of many sites, but in
   particular those along the Welsh borders. The reader is referred for
   further information, to the recent publication: Fenn's and Whixall
   Mosses, edited by Berry, Gale, Daniels, and Allmark, 1996.},
