Note = {17th Annual World Environmental and Water Resources Congress,
   Sacramento, CA, MAY 21-25, 2017},
Organization = {Amer Soc Civil Engineers; Amer Soc Civil Engineers, Environm \& Water
   Resources Inst},
Abstract = {Secondary settling tanks (SSTs) or secondary clarifiers are integral to
   the performance of the activated sludge process which is the most
   commonly used biological method of wastewater treatment. The SST's
   purpose is to separate the biosolids from and return them to the
   reactor. SST research was first performed more than 100 years ago and
   many significant advances have been made. CFD clarifier models were
   introduced in 1970s and fully developed in the 1980s and 1990s. Recent
   CFD models have provided new insight and better SST designs. To continue
   this progress we studied the major influential SST design factors using
   a numerical Quasi 3-D SST model to verify several published SST studies.
   The papers provided the input and design parameters for the model, such
   as tank geometry, loading rates and density currents to determine their
   effect efficiency. Model outputs were compared to field data and
   simulation results in the original papers. Our results showed
   differences and similarities between the Quasi 3-D model and the
   simulation outcomes in the published papers. The Quasi 3-D model showed
   many opposite trends in efficiency as a function of loading rates.
   Future work is focusing on (1) the development of a systematic and
   repeatable rheological measurement protocol, (2) the development of a
   more reliable rheological model under the guidance of this protocol, and
   (3) the development of a new 3D clarifier model coupled with the new
   rheological model.},
